ll start with the ``HTTP_`` prefix,\n    46→                as in: ``falcon.HTTP_204``. (See also: :ref:`status`.)\n    47→\n    48→        status_code (int): HTTP status code normalized from :attr:`status`.\n    49→            When a code is assigned to this property, :attr:`status` is updated,\n    50→            and vice-versa. The status code can be useful when needing to check\n    51→            in middleware for codes that fall into a certain class, e.g.::\n    52→\n    53→                if resp.status_code >= 400:\n    54→                    log.warning(f'returning error response: {resp.status_code}')\n    55→\n    56→        media (object): A serializable object supported by the media handlers\n    57→            configured via :class:`falcon.RequestOptions`.\n    58→\n    59→            Note:\n    60→                See also :ref:`media` for more information regarding media\n    61→                handling.\n    62→\n    63→        text (str): String representing response content.\n    64→\n    65→            Note:\n    66→                Falcon will encode the given text as UTF-8\n    67→                in the response. If the content is already a byte string,\n    68→                use the :attr:`data` attribute instead (it's faster).\n    69→\n    70→        data (bytes): Byte string representing response content.\n    71→\n    72→            Use this attribute in lieu of `text` when your content is\n    73→            already a byte string (of type ``bytes``).\n    74→\n    75→            Warning:\n    76→                Always use the `text` attribute for text, or encode it\n    77→                first to ``bytes`` when using the `data` attribute, to\n    78→                ensure Unicode characters are properly encoded in the\n    79→                HTTP response.\n    80→\n    81→        stream: An async iterator or generator that yields a series of\n    82→            byte strings that will be streamed to the ASGI server as a\n    83→            series of \"http.response.body\" events. Falcon will assume the\n    84→            body is complete when the iterable is exhausted or as soon as it\n    85→            yields ``None`` rather than an instance of ``bytes``::\n    86→\n    87→                async def producer():\n    88→                    while True:\n    89→                        data_chunk = await read_data()\n    90→                        if not data_chunk:\n    91→                            break\n    92→\n    93→                        yield data_chunk\n    94→\n    95→                resp.stream = producer\n    96→\n    97→            Alternatively, a file-like object may be used as long as it\n    98→            implements an awaitable ``read()`` method::\n    99→\n   100→                resp.stream = await aiofiles.open('resp_data.bin', 'rb')\n   101→\n   102→            If the object assigned to :py:attr:`~.stream` holds any resources\n   103→            (such as a file handle) that must be explicitly released, the\n   104→            object must implement a ``close()`` method. The ``close()`` method\n   105→            will be called after exhausting the iterable or file-like object.\n   106→\n   107→            Note:\n   108→                In order to be compatible with Python 3.7+ and PEP 479,\n   109→                async iterators must return ``None`` instead of raising\n   110→                :py:class:`StopIteration`. This requirement does not\n   111→                apply to async generators (PEP 525).\n   112→\n   113→            Note:\n   114→                If the stream length is known in advance, you may wish to\n   115→                also set the Content-Length header on the response.\n   116→\n   117→        sse (coroutine): A Server-Sent Event (SSE) emitter, implemented as\n   118→            an async iterator or generator that yields a series of\n   119→            of :py:class:`falcon.asgi.SSEvent` instances. Each event will be\n   120→            serialized and sent