  42\u2192\n    43\u2192            Note:\n    44\u2192                The Falcon framework itself provides a number of constants for\n    45\u2192                common status codes. They all start with the ``HTTP_`` prefix,\n    46\u2192                as in: ``falcon.HTTP_204``. (See also: :ref:`status`.)\n    47\u2192\n    48\u2192        status_code (int): HTTP status code normalized from :attr:`status`.\n    49\u2192            When a code is assigned to this property, :attr:`status` is updated,\n    50\u2192            and vice-versa. The status code can be useful when needing to check\n    51\u2192            in middleware for codes that fall into a certain class, e.g.::\n    52\u2192\n    53\u2192                if resp.status_code >= 400:\n    54\u2192                    log.warning(f'returning error response: {resp.status_code}')\n    55\u2192\n    56\u2192        media (object): A serializable object supported by the media handlers\n    57\u2192            configured via :class:`falcon.RequestOptions`.\n    58\u2192\n    59\u2192            Note:\n    60\u2192                See also :ref:`media` for more information regarding media\n    61\u2192                handling.\n    62\u2192\n    63\u2192        text (str): String representing response content.\n    64\u2192\n    65\u2192            Note:\n    66\u2192                Falcon will encode the given text as UTF-8\n    67\u2192                in the response. If the content is already a byte string,\n    68\u2192                use the :attr:`data` attribute instead (it's faster).\n    69\u2192\n    70\u2192        data (bytes): Byte string representing response content.\n    71\u2192\n    72\u2192            Use this attribute in lieu of `text` when your content is\n    73\u2192            already a byte string (of type ``bytes``).\n    74\u2192\n    75\u2192            Warning:\n    76\u2192                Always use the `text` attribute for text, or encode it\n    77\u2192                first to