pply to async generators (PEP 525).\n   112\u2192\n   113\u2192            Note:\n   114\u2192                If the stream length is known in advance, you may wish to\n   115\u2192                also set the Content-Length header on the response.\n   116\u2192\n   117\u2192        sse (coroutine): A Server-Sent Event (SSE) emitter, implemented as\n   118\u2192            an async iterator or generator that yields a series of\n   119\u2192            of :py:class:`falcon.asgi.SSEvent` instances. Each event will be\n   120\u2192            serialized and sent to the client as HTML5 Server-Sent Events::\n   121\u2192\n   122\u2192                async def emitter():\n   123\u2192                    while True:\n   124\u2192                        some_event = await get_next_event()\n   125\u2192\n   126\u2192                        if not some_event:\n   127\u2192                            # Send an event consisting of a single \"ping\"\n   128\u2192                            #   comment to keep the connection alive.\n   129\u2192                            yield SSEvent()\n   130\u2192\n   131\u2192                            # Alternatively, one can simply yield None and\n   132\u2192                            #   a \"ping\" will also be sent as above.\n   133\u2192\n   134\u2192                            # yield\n   135\u2192\n   136\u2192                            continue\n   137\u2192\n   138\u2192                        yield SSEvent(json=some_event, retry=5000)\n   139\u2192\n   140\u2192                        # ...or\n   141\u2192\n   142\u2192                        yield SSEvent(data=b'something', event_id=some_id)\n   143\u2192\n   144\u2192                        # Alternatively, you may yield anything that implements\n   145\u2192                        #   a serialize() method that returns a byte string\n   146\u2192                        #   conforming to the SSE event stream format.\n   147\u2192\n   148\u2192                        # yield some_event\n   149\u2192\n   150\u2192                resp.sse = emitter()\n   151\u2192\n   152\u2192            Note:\n   153\u2192                When the `sse` property is set, it supersedes both the\n   154\u2192                `text` and `data` properties.\n   155\u2192\n   156\u2192            Note:\n   157\u2192                When hosting an app that emits Server-Sent Events, the web\n   158\u2192                server should be set with a relatively long keep-alive TTL to\n   159\u2192                minimize the overhead of connection renegotiations.\n   160\u2192\n   161\u2192        context (object): Empty object to hold any data (in its attributes)\n   162\u2192            about the response which is specific to your app (e.g. session\n   163\u2192            object). Falcon itself will not interact with this attribute after\n   164\u2192            it has been initialized.\n   165\u2192\n   166\u2192            Note:\n   167\u2192                The preferred way to pass response-specific data, when using the\n   168\u2192                default context type, is to set attributes directly on the\n   169\u2192                `context` object. For example::\n   170\u2192\n   171\u2192                    resp.context.cache_strategy = 'lru'\n   172\u2192\n   173\u2192        context_type (class): Class variable that determines the factory or\n   174\u2192            type to use for initializing the `context` attribute. By default,\n   175\u2192            the framework will instantiate bare objects (instances of the bare\n   176\u2192            :class:`falcon.Context` class). However, you may override this\n   177\u2192            behavior by creating a custom child class of\n   178\u2192            :class:`falcon.asgi.Response`, and then passing that new class\n   179\u2192            to ``falcon.App()`` by way of the latter's `response_type`\n   180\u2192            parameter.\n   181\u2192\n   182\u2192            Note:\n   183\u2192                When